lope of the function $f(x)$ is negative when\n\n$$\nx\\in (\\,-\\infty ,-6\\,)⋃(-1,3\\,).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507406 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507406 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507406 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507406 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/56/56a341e4982e2b91772399c81b9cef0df394320b1c640bec43ab0f39ab973143.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of a continuous function $f(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $f(x)$ has a local extremum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ are shown in the picture." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507406 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19ac6220-7276-52db-bb25-1688d9b81ad8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507406 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${r}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=2$ and $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507411 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/40/407f8bae5534f4d